  "translation": {
    "literal": "My old friend takes his leave of Yellow Crane Tower in the west; in the misty blossom-time of the third month he goes down to Yangzhou. The lone sail's far-off shadow disappears into the blue emptiness; I see only the Yangtze flowing toward the edge of the sky.",
    "poetic": "My old friend leaves Yellow Crane Tower in the west, bound for Yangzhou in the blossoming haze of spring. His lone sail dwindles to a speck and is lost in the blue, and only the Yangtze is left, flowing to the horizon."
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "journey",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "A friend's journey (about 730)",
      "body": "Li Bai saw his older friend Meng Haoran off from Yellow Crane Tower, in present-day Wuhan, as Meng sailed east down the Yangtze to Yangzhou. The poem is dated to about 730. Yellow Crane Tower has been destroyed and rebuilt many times; the tower seen today dates from 1985. 西 in the first line refers to where the tower stands: Wuhan is west of Yangzhou."
    },
    {
      "id": "smoke",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "煙花 means mist and blossoms",
      "body": "煙花 in the second line has nothing to do with fireworks. It means the haze (煙) and flowers (花) of late spring. Li Bai's eyes then follow the sail until it disappears. All that is left is the river, which keeps flowing toward the horizon, so the poem ends on empty space and the friend's absence."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 樓 · 州 · 流",
      "body": "Lines one, two and four end in 樓 (lóu), 州 (zhōu) and 流 (liú), all in the 尤 rhyme. Line three ends in 盡 and does not rhyme."
    },
    {
      "id": "readings",
      "kind": "other",
      "title": "Words with more than one reading",
      "body": "長江 is read Chángjiāng (long river) and 長 is cháng, not zhǎng. 盡 is read jìn, in the falling tone. 下 means \"to go down\", here down a river."
    }
  ],
